      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Asthma has a growing prevalence all over the world. GINA guidelines had improved diagnostic and therapeutic asthma approach, but it is not enough."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the knowledge about GINA in physicians of first, second and third care level."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Physicians working in first, second and third care level, from medical units related to the Centro Medico Nacional La Raza answered a 19 items questionnaire about GINA. Results were analyzed with Kruskal-Wallis, Pearson's correlation and U of Mann-Whitney tests."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "We included 179 physicians with an age average of 43 years; 99 or them were 99 females and 80 males. According to medical specialty, Allergy and Immunology had 145 points; Pneumology, 136; Pediatrics, 122; Family Medicine, 81 and Internal Medicine, 78. We found a statistical difference between these specialties (p < 0.0001). Doctors from third and second care level had a higher and significative scoring than first care level colleagues (p < 0.005). We did not find differences between labor years, working schedule and gender with GINA knowledge scoring."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "There are differences in GINA knowledge between medical specialties and care level."
        }
      ]},
